{
  "requirement": "An outline language can additionally supply the syntax capability used by the\nSDK's [structural search](sources.md#outline-and-search-the-available-files).\nThe operation returns matched byte ranges within the supplied source:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait OutlineLang: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    fn search_structure(\n        &self,\n        source: &str,\n        rule: &serde_json::Map<String, serde_json::Value>,\n    ) -> Result<Vec<std::ops::Range<usize>>, OutlineError>;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nThe default implementation returns an unsupported-language error. Plain text\nsupplies outlining only.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no test-adequacy gap for `requirements.sdk.outlines.language_provider.structural_search`. Three obligations follow from the text, and the tests cover each one.\n\n1. **Matched byte ranges within the supplied source.**\n   - `outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_structural_search_returns_matches_and_rejects_bad_rules_and_source]` runs a `function_item` rule against Rust source with two functions. It asserts that the returned ranges slice to exactly `fn first() {}` and `fn second() {}`. That checks both that the ranges are byte ranges into the supplied source and that they are the right matches.\n   - The same test checks that a malformed rule and unparsable source both return errors.\n   - `outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_claim_extensions_and_supply_the_same_public_outline]` has an inner `check` helper that runs a `regex` rule on every bundled language: Rust, Python, TypeScript, Tsx, Markdown, Css, Toml and Yaml. It asserts that the result is non-empty and that every range satisfies `source.get(range).is_some()`, so each range is in bounds and on a character boundary.\n   - Only Rust has an exact-match assertion. The obligation is the same for every language, and the other languages still get bounds and non-empty checks, so I don't count this as a gap.\n\n2. **The default implementation returns an unsupported-language error.**\n   - `outline_content.rs#fn[outline_only_languages_report_unsupported_structural_search]` calls `search_structure` on `CustomLanguage`. I checked that `impl[CustomLanguage/OutlineLang]` doesn't override `search_structure`, so the call uses the trait default.\n   - The test asserts that the call returns an `Err` whose message contains \"unsupported\".\n\n3. **Plain text supplies outlining only.** The same test calls `languages::PlainText.search_structure` and asserts that it returns an error whose message contains \"plain-text\". That shows plain text gives no structural search. The bundled-language test also asserts the `PlainText` name and that it claims no extensions.\n\nThe trait signature (`&str` source, JSON rule map, `Result<Vec<Range<usize>>, OutlineError>`) is exercised by every call above and is enforced by compilation.\n\nAll three tests carry `{test}` references to the subject, so there are no marking gaps.",
